    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/faculty-appointment-in-international-security-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Faculty Appointment in International Security",
      "description": "Faculty Appointment in International Security - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Faculty Appointment in International Security Stanford University Policy Institute Fellows Opening at: Sep 29 2026 - 9:00am PDT Add to favorites View favorites Stanford University seeks an expert in international security affairs to be appointed as Senior Fellow in the Freeman Spogli Institute for International Studies at the Center for International Security and Cooperation (CISAC). Faculty from the social sciences are particularly encouraged to apply. Senior Fellows at Stanford University are full members of the Academic Council, with a rank equivalent to tenured associate or full professor. The successful candidate for this position will be an internationally recognized scholar with an interest and expertise in contemporary threats and challenges in the domain of international security affairs. They may well have other significant interests and expertise as well that would add to Stanford’s faculty community working on a variety of security issues. In addition, they may also have a record of policy relevant publication, teaching, or public service. Applicants should possess an exceptional record of research and publication and be committed to teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels and training pre- and post-doctoral fellows at CISAC to become the next generation of scholars on international security policy. Applicants might also have substantial interest or proven expertise in making, advising or implementing national or international security policy. The successful candidates will be expected to provide leadership for the study of international security issues at Stanford University. Application packages should be submitted online and include the following documents: 1) a curriculum vitae with complete bibliography; 2) a research statement (up to 2 pages); 3) a statement of teaching interests (1 page); 4) up to three examples of scholarly publications; and 5) a cover letter. Review of application files will start from October 19, 2026. Questions regarding this application may be directed to Patrick Laboon . Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The expected base pay range for this position is: Senior Fellow: $186,000 - $285,000 Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including years since terminal degree, training, and field or discipline; departmental budget availability; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/medical-director-of-the-adult-emergency-department-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Medical Director of the Adult Emergency Department",
      "description": "Medical Director of the Adult Emergency Department - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Medical Director of the Adult Emergency Department Stanford University CE - Clinician Educator Opening at: Sep 23 2026 - 9:00am PDT Closing at: Sep 22 2027 - 11:55pm PDT Add to favorites View favorites The Department of Emergency Medicine at Stanford University School of Medicine seeks an exceptional Board-Certified Emergency Medicine physician to serve as Medical Director of the Adult Emergency Department . This leadership position includes a full-time faculty appointment in the Clinician Educator Line. Academic rank will be commensurate with experience and qualifications. Candidates at the Clinical Assistant, Clinical Associate, and Clinical Professor levels will be considered, with preference for individuals who qualify for appointment at the Clinical Associate Professor or Clinical Professor level. Stanford Emergency Medicine is internationally recognized for its commitment to precision emergency medicine, leveraging data, technology, and innovation to improve the care of individual patients and communities. Key areas of focus include advancing access to care, digital health and artificial intelligence, human-centered data science, population and global health, and the redesign of medical education. We seek a physician leader who shares this vision and is committed to advancing the future of emergency care delivery through operational excellence, innovation, and collaboration. The Adult Medical Director serves as a key member of the Department's Clinical Operations Leadership Team and is responsible for providing physician leadership for Stanford's Adult Emergency Department, a modern 66-bed emergency department with an integrated Clinical Decision Unit located within a state-of-the-art academic medical center. Working closely with the Vice Chair of Clinical Operations, hospital leadership, nursing leaders, quality and informatics teams, and departmental faculty leaders, the Medical Director will help shape the strategic direction of emergency care delivery across Stanford Health Care. The successful candidate will lead efforts to optimize patient access, flow, capacity management, quality, safety, and patient experience while fostering a culture of physician engagement, professionalism, collaboration, and continuous improvement. The Medical Director will represent Emergency Medicine within interdisciplinary and health system leadership forums and will partner with colleagues across Adult and Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Critical Care Medicine, Observation Medicine, EMS, Digital Health, and affiliated regional clinical sites to advance an integrated clinical enterprise. We are particularly interested in candidates with demonstrated expertise in one or more areas relevant to modern emergency care delivery, including emergency department operations, patient flow and throughput, quality and process improvement, clinical informatics, digital health, data science, clinical decision support, innovative care models, artificial intelligence, or health system transformation. Clinical responsibilities include providing care in the emergency department at Stanford Hospital, a world-renowned academic medical center with approximately 90,000 adult emergency department visits annually. Stanford Emergency Medicine includes more than 120 board-certified emergency physicians with broad scholarly interests and substantial extramural funding. The department sponsors 13 fellowship programs, a four-year residency program, required and advanced medical student clerkships, and a wide range of educational programs across Stanford University. This position offers an exceptional opportunity to lead one of the nation's premier emergency departments at a time of continued growth, innovation, and transformation in emergency care. We invite you to apply and join us in our mission to shape the future of emergency medicine operations at Stanford University and beyond. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The Department of Emergency Medicine, School of Medicine, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. The expected base pay for this position is: Clinical Assistant Professor: $328,000 - $364,000 Clinical Associate Professor: $375,000 - $393,000 Clinical Professor: $405,000 - $420,000 This pay range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide-range of benefits , including housing assistance , please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. The major criterion for appointment as Clinician Educators is excellence in the overall mix of clinical care, teaching, administrative and/or scholarship appropriate to the programmatic need the individual is expected to fulfill. Academic rank and line will be determined by the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. Please submit a letter of interest and curriculum vitae to: https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/medical-director-adult-emergency-department-stanford-university-california-united-states . For questions or additional information, please contact John Kendall, MD, at emedacademicaffairs@stanford.edu . Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/cancer-pain-program-director-division-of-pain-medicine-stanford-university-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Cancer Pain Program Director",
      "description": "Cancer Pain Program Director – Division of Pain Medicine, Stanford University - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Cancer Pain Program Director – Division of Pain Medicine, Stanford University Stanford University CE - Clinician Educator Opening at: Sep 2 2025 - 4:00pm PDT Add to favorites View favorites Job Opportunity: Cancer Pain Program Director – Division of Pain Medicine, Stanford University The Division of Pain Medicine, within the Department of Anesthesiology, Perioperative and Pain Medicine at Stanford University, is seeking an outstanding Cancer Pain Program Director to join our expanding Pain Service Line, with an appointment in the Clinician Educator Line (Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor). This is an exceptional opportunity to join a vibrant, interdisciplinary team at one of the world’s leading academic institutions, where innovation in pain care, education, and research is central to our mission. The primary criterion for appointment as a Clinician Educator is excellence in the overall mix of clinical care, teaching, administrative leadership, and/or scholarship aligned with programmatic needs. The successful candidate will serve as the Cancer Pain Program Director , providing both clinical and programmatic leadership in Cancer Pain Medicine. This role will expand access to specialized oncologic pain care across outpatient services, procedural interventions, and inpatient consults, while also advancing the integration of behavioral and multidisciplinary approaches to cancer pain management. The Director will lead program development, enhance care pathways, and strengthen coordination across oncology, palliative care, psychology, and interventional pain services to improve access, reduce treatment delays, and optimize patient outcomes. Stanford is internationally recognized for excellence in integrated, patient-centered care and for advancing the science of pain management. As demand for cancer care continues to grow, this position will address a critical unmet need while advancing Stanford’s national leadership in multidisciplinary, innovative cancer pain management, and supporting the institution’s teaching and research missions. Why Join Stanford? Be part of a nationally leading program in pain medicine, housed within a world-class academic medical center. Collaborate with top clinicians and researchers in pain medicine, anesthesiology, psychiatry, neurology, rehabilitation, and behavioral science. Work in a multidisciplinary environment with access to cutting-edge treatments and research in pain management. Contribute to the growth of behavioral health services in pain care and shape innovative, evidence-based models for integrated treatment. Key Responsibilities Provide expert consultation and treatment for patients with cancer-related pain in both inpatient and outpatient settings. Collaborate with oncology, palliative care, and interventional pain colleagues to ensure seamless, comprehensive care. Perform advanced outpatient pain procedures to enhance patient outcomes and reduce care delays. Contribute to clinical education of fellows, residents, and medical students in cancer pain management. Participate in clinical or translational research initiatives related to oncologic pain. Qualifications MD or equivalent, with fellowship training in Pain Medicine. Board certification or eligibility in Anesthesiology and Pain Medicine (or relevant specialty). Demonstrated excellence in clinical care of complex pain patients, with experience in oncology or palliative settings preferred. Strong teaching and mentoring skills. Commitment to interdisciplinary and patient-centered care. Additional Opportunities Leadership: Serve as faculty lead in the development of a Cancer Pain subspecialty program within the Division of Pain Medicine. Professional Growth: Opportunities for advancement in academic rank and leadership within the Division and Department. Collaboration: Work closely with Stanford Cancer Institute, palliative care, and interventional services to drive innovation. This is an exciting opportunity to join a leading academic pain medicine team at Stanford and play a key role in advancing cancer pain care. The Cancer Pain Program Director will contribute to Stanford’s mission of delivering innovative, multidisciplinary care and improving the quality of life for patients with complex oncologic pain conditions. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The Anesthesiology Department, School of Medicine, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. To apply, submit CV and a brief cover letter. For questions, please contact Ranjini Deo at rreddy1@stanford.edu . The expected base pay range for this position is: Clinical Assistant Professor:  $465,000–475,000 Clinical Associate Professor:  $486,000–$496,000 Clinical Professor:  $504,000–$525,000 This pay range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide-range of benefits , including housing assistance , please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/assistant-or-untenured-associate-professor-in-electrical-engineering-stanford-university-california-united-states-fdec0dd4-db8e-4f93-9e33-239d1e5dfd29": {
      "title": "Assistant or Untenured Associate Professor in Electrical Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant or Untenured Associate Professor in Electrical Engineering - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Assistant or Untenured Associate Professor in Electrical Engineering Stanford University University Tenure Line Opening at: Oct 6 2026 - 9:00am PDT Closing at: Dec 13 2026 - 11:55pm PST Add to favorites View favorites Stanford University Department of Electrical Engineering Faculty Opening In the application system, within the field for “Primary Research Area for Position*” SELECT: Electrical Engineering The Department of Electrical Engineering at Stanford University invites applications for a tenure track faculty position at the junior level (Assistant or untenured Associate Professor). We invite applications from individuals working at the frontiers of electrical and computer engineering, broadly defined. While we give highest priority to the overall originality and promise of the candidate's work, particular areas of interest in this search include research that combines information sciences and physical devices to drive new applications. Please visit our website for more information about the EE Department at https://ee.stanford.edu . Applicants must have completed, or be completing, a Ph.D. in the advertised field or a closely related one. Evidence of the ability to pursue a program of research, and a strong commitment to graduate and undergraduate teaching are also required. A successful candidate will be expected to teach courses at the graduate and undergraduate levels and to build and lead a team of graduate students in PhD research. Applications should include a resume, brief statements of research and teaching interests (3-5 pages total for both combined), and the names and e-mail addresses of 3-5 references. We will request letters of recommendation upon submission of the candidate’s application. The Electrical Engineering Department, School of Engineering, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. Applications will be accepted until 11:59PM on Sunday, December 13, 2026. Interviews may begin in the fall term. Applicants are encouraged to apply early. The expected base pay range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $144,100- $163,766 Associate Professor (untenured): $158,510 - $196,803 This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment and does not include summer salary. For more information about compensation and our wide range of benefits, including housing assistance, please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including years since terminal degree, training, and field or discipline; departmental budget availability; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Back to all jobs Apply Now First Name (required) 9ae5ee8c Last Name (required) b62b5583 Email (required) 90c55d5a Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 44db3bd5 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. School School of Engineering School of Engineering Line University Tenure Line University Tenure Line Location Stanford University, California, United States Stanford University, California, United States First Name (required) 357f482c Last Name (required) 4f35cdfd Email (required) af7a7946 Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
