    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/director-of-the-general-psychiatry-residency-program-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Director of the General Psychiatry Residency Program",
      "description": "Director of the General Psychiatry Residency Program - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Director of the General Psychiatry Residency Program Stanford University CE - Clinician Educator University Medical Line Opening at: Jul 9 2026 - 9:00am PDT Closing at: Jun 30 2027 - 11:55pm PDT Add to favorites View favorites The Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences at Stanford University School of Medicine is seeking a new Director for the General Psychiatry Residency Program. The successful candidate will be appointed at the rank of Associate or full Professor in the University Medical Line or Clinician Educator Line, with the exact academic rank and line to be determined by the qualifications and experience of the selected candidate. The General Psychiatry Residency is a 4-year program is approved by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) that strives to develop a standard of clinical excellence for the 21st-century practice of psychiatry by integrating scientific advancement, clinical artistry, and an understanding of the complex needs of the psychiatric population. The program provides its residents with an outstanding and balanced educational experience through emphasizing biological, psychological, and sociocultural aspects of psychiatry. Professional growth in academic, leadership, administrative, and clinical experiences is fostered through intensive mentoring and exposure to customized experiences based on resident interest. The program also encourages self-reflective learning and enables personal growth through an emphasis on mutual and supportive feedback mechanisms and opportunities for furthering self-awareness. The selected candidate will be responsible for providing strategic, educational, and administrative leadership of the residency program and for performing key administrative oversight/liaison functions for the advanced physician fellowship programs of the department, such as Addiction Medicine, Child and Adolescent Psychiatry, Forensic Psychiatry, Psychosomatic Medicine, and Sleep Medicine, among others. This leadership role ensures compliance with ACGME standards, fosters a supportive learning environment, and promotes innovation in graduate medical education. Duties include oversight of all aspects of residency program administration and curriculum development, recruitment, mentoring, and evaluation of residents and faculty. The selected candidate will also lead program evaluation and continuous quality improvement initiatives, promote a culture of professionalism, wellness, and inclusivity, and collaborate with institutional leadership to align program goals with organizational mission. Applicants must have a medical degree, have completed training in General Psychiatry, be board-certified in General Psychiatry, and possess or be fully eligible for a California medical license. The chosen candidate will have demonstrated experience in graduate medical education and leadership, strong interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills, and commitment to teaching, mentorship, and academic excellence. The major criteria for appointment for faculty in the University Medical Line is excellence in the overall mix of clinical care, clinical teaching, scholarly activity that advances clinical medicine, and institutional service appropriate to the programmatic need the individual is expected to fulfill. The major criterion for appointment as Clinician Educators is excellence in the overall mix of clinical care, teaching, administrative and/or scholarship appropriate to the programmatic need the individual is expected to fulfill. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu. The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, School of Medicine, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. The expected pay range for this position is: Associate Professor: $260,000-272,000 Full Professor: $290,000-350,000 This pay range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide range of benefits, including housing assistance, please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Applicants should submit their curriculum vitae, a brief letter outlining their interests and the names of three references via the application link. Please contact Heather Kenna Hall (hkenna@stanford.edu) with any questions. Back to all jobs Apply Now First Name (required) 4694d965 Last Name (required) ebd2f391 Email (required) b000355d Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 16bc6b38 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. School School of Medicine School of Medicine Line CE - Clinician Educator University Medical Line CE - Clinician Educator University Medical Line Location Stanford University, California, United States Stanford University, California, United States First Name (required) a752b0c2 Last Name (required) 54e1e196 Email (required) ea252c98 Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford 